Derek Ray wrote:
> 
> Robert Shaw wrote:
> 
>>Rubbing non rusty armour could polish it, giving a 5% chance of
>>reflection, once only.
> 
> 
> A more proper balance would be to confine this to non-rusty
> non-enchanted standard iron/bronze body armour (this means no dragon
> scales, mithril, crystal, or +1 ring mails) -- and have the reflection
> status not be known to the polisher until something reflectable strikes
> it.  Yes, you could still zap yourself with a ray-type wand to determine
> reflective status; this of course has a number of additional costs
> related to determining that your armour has NOT gained the reflection
> intrinsic.


I didn't mean intrinsic. I meant something more like greased,
which can wear off. Polish up the armour, and it'll reflect
one spell, maybe two, before losing its shine. Alternately,
it has a chance of getting scuffed everytime you're hit,
averaging perhaps a dozen turns.

Since this is much weaker than the intrinsic, though not
entirely useless, it can safely be relatively easy to do.

In the version where polishing can give armour the reflection
intrinsic permanently, I'd go for the same kind of restrictions
you're suggesting.

